WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.700
A growth factor stays outside the cell.

2
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.180
Why does it take three kinases in a row to tell the nucleus?

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.640
The receptor sits at the surface, yet the cell's genes lie deep inside.

4
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.080
Carrying that order across the cytoplasm takes a carefully built relay.

5
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:26.663
First, the active receptor recruits Grb2 and SOS to the membrane.

6
00:00:28.183 --> 00:00:34.023
SOS makes membrane-anchored Ras drop GDP, so plentiful GTP binds instead.

7
00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:36.643
Ras is not a kinase.

8
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:43.283
Bound GTP pulls two flexible switch loops taut, making a surface Raf can grip.

9
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:46.447
Active Ras acts by contact.

10
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:52.387
It catches folded, resting Raf from the cytosol and holds Raf at the membrane.

11
00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:55.567
Binding alone does not open Raf.

12
00:00:56.847 --> 00:01:00.507
Contact with membrane lipids and further steps help it unfold.

13
00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:07.327
Two Raf kinase domains then pair side to side, which normal activation requires.

14
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:12.737
Paired Raf phosphorylates the middle kinase, MEK, on two serines.

15
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.457
MEK is unusual among protein kinases.

16
00:01:17.937 --> 00:01:21.937
Most kinases target either serine and threonine, or tyrosine alone.

17
00:01:23.577 --> 00:01:25.537
MEK is a dual-specificity kinase.

18
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:31.657
It tags ERK on a threonine and a tyrosine, one residue apart, creating a

19
00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:32.717
two-phosphate lock.

20
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.067
Pause and predict.

21
00:01:36.747 --> 00:01:41.167
Suppose a phosphatase removes just one of ERK's two phosphates, leaving the other

22
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.167
phosphate attached.

23
00:01:43.547 --> 00:01:46.167
Does ERK stay half-active, or shut off completely?

24
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.760
ERK shuts off completely.

25
00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.260
Removing either phosphate silences it, because ERK requires both to align its active

26
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:06.620
site. In the test tube, if one hit has chance p, two independent hits have chance p

27
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:12.700
squared. So one in ten becomes one in a hundred, and one half becomes one quarter.

28
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.420
At low MEK, p squared stays tiny, then climbs steeply.

29
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.990
Three kinases in series do more than carry a message.

30
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:28.510
Each tier can amplify the signal, sharpen its rise, and open control points for

31
00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:29.990
phosphatases and feedback.

32
00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.130
MEK is a narrow pipe aimed almost only at ERK.

33
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.290
Active ERK then fans out widely across cytoplasm and nucleus.

34
00:02:40.433 --> 00:02:45.153
In the nucleus, ERK phosphorylates Elk-1, which helps switch on c-fos.

35
00:02:46.373 --> 00:02:50.833
That is an immediate early gene, one of the first genes on, within minutes.

36
00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:54.977
So this three-tier relay is no wasteful detour.

37
00:02:56.497 --> 00:02:58.957
Ras gripping Raf starts the chain at the membrane.

38
00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:05.257
Three kinases in series can then amplify, sharpen, and control that signal.

39
00:03:06.657 --> 00:03:09.117
Finally, ERK fans out to proteins and genes.
